1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,210
In the nineteenth century, European empires swallowed almost all of South Asia.

2
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:09,350
India, Burma and Ceylon all came under British rule.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:14,280
Further east, the French and the Dutch carved up much of Southeast Asia.

4
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:19,740
But one small mountain kingdom, squeezed right in the middle, was never colonized.

5
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:23,470
That kingdom was Nepal. So how did it survive?

6
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:30,280
The answer involves a king's warning, a brutal war, and a deal that still shapes Nepal today.

7
00:00:30,730 --> 00:00:35,850
Our story begins in the small hill kingdom of Gorkha, in the seventeen-forties.

8
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:43,450
Its young king, Prithvi Narayan Shah, set out to unite dozens of rival hill states into one country.

9
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:49,450
Gorkha was small and poor, but its hill soldiers were tough, and its king was patient.

10
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:53,240
It took him about twenty-five years of campaigns.

11
00:00:53,690 --> 00:01:03,610
In seventeen sixty-eight and sixty-nine, his army took the rich cities of the Kathmandu Valley, and Kathmandu became the capital of a new nation.

12
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:07,970
Prithvi Narayan Shah left his court a famous piece of advice.

13
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:12,340
Nepal, he said, is like a yam between two boulders.

14
00:01:12,790 --> 00:01:19,980
On one side, the Qing Empire of China. On the other, the East India Company, growing stronger every year.

15
00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:26,900
A yam between two rocks has to be careful. Push too hard against either side, and it gets crushed.

16
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:35,240
That image stuck. Nepali leaders and writers still quote it today, whenever their two giant neighbours compete for influence.

17
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:38,700
His successors did not always stay careful.

18
00:01:39,150 --> 00:01:46,490
Gorkha armies kept expanding, west into Kumaon and Garhwal, and south into the fertile plains of the Terai.

19
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:52,480
Along that southern frontier, Nepal's land now pressed right against Company territory.

20
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:57,800
Disputes over border land turned into open war in eighteen fourteen.

21
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:02,090
The Company expected an easy win. It did not get one.

22
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:12,950
At a small hill fort called Nalapani, about six hundred Gorkha soldiers under Captain Balbhadra Kunwar faced some three thousand five hundred Company troops.

23
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:19,710
On the very first day, the British commander, Major General Robert Gillespie, was shot and killed.

24
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:23,730
The garrison held out for a month, then slipped away at night.

25
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:28,170
Across the hills, the Company met the same fierce resistance.

26
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:34,400
Supplies had to be carried up steep ridges, and every fort had to be taken one by one.

27
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:37,810
The war dragged on into eighteen sixteen.

28
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:41,310
In the end, the Company's larger army won.

29
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:49,910
Under the Treaty of Sugauli, Nepal gave up nearly a third of its territory, including Kumaon, Garhwal and much of the Terai.

30
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:56,700
The treaty was agreed in December eighteen fifteen and ratified in early eighteen sixteen.

31
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:02,010
It had to accept a British resident in Kathmandu, and a new, fixed border.

32
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:06,860
Later that same year, the British even handed back part of the Terai.

33
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:12,790
Nepal had lost the war. But it kept its king, its capital, and its independence.

34
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:16,290
So why didn't the British simply take the rest?

35
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:23,120
First, the mountains. Fighting in Nepal's hills had already proven slow, costly and deadly.

36
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:29,010
Second, they had already won what they wanted: the disputed lowlands, and a settled border.

37
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:35,220
And third, the war had taught them something surprising. They respected the men who had fought them.

38
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:41,000
After the war, the British began recruiting Nepali hillmen into their own army.

39
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:44,650
They became known around the world as the Gurkhas.

40
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:50,050
Nepal kept its freedom, and Britain gained soldiers it deeply respected.

41
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:56,580
In eighteen forty-six, power in Kathmandu passed to the Rana family of prime ministers.

42
00:03:57,030 --> 00:04:00,910
The Ranas chose friendship with Britain over confrontation.

43
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:09,790
In eighteen fifty-seven, when rebellion swept across India, Nepal sent troops to help the British, and Gurkhas fought at Lucknow.

44
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:16,240
In the First World War, more than ninety thousand Gurkhas served alongside British forces.

45
00:04:16,690 --> 00:04:21,190
More than twenty thousand of them were killed, wounded or went missing.

46
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:25,750
In return, Nepal wanted one thing above all: recognition.

47
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:34,230
On the twenty-first of December, nineteen twenty-three, Britain and Nepal signed a treaty at Singha Durbar in Kathmandu.

48
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:42,610
It was signed by the British envoy, Lieutenant Colonel William O'Connor, and Nepal's prime minister, Chandra Shumsher Rana.

49
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:47,710
It formally recognized Nepal as a sovereign, independent country.

50
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,750
Nepal celebrated with a two-day national holiday.

51
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:54,270
But was Nepal truly free?

52
00:04:54,720 --> 00:05:04,530
Historians point out that for more than a century, Britain kept heavy influence over Nepal's foreign relations, and even over the weapons it could buy.

53
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:10,650
One British official described Nepal as a glorified member of the protectorate.

54
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:18,800
And some argue that Nepal's independence was partly paid for with its young men, sent to fight in Britain's wars.

55
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:23,990
Nepal was never a colony. But it lived in the shadow of an empire.

56
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:32,360
So Nepal survived through a mix of things: hard mountains, hard fighters, and careful deals with a much bigger power.

57
00:05:32,810 --> 00:05:35,960
The yam between two boulders was never crushed.

58
00:05:36,410 --> 00:05:42,560
Today, Gurkhas still serve in the British Army, more than two hundred years after Nalapani.

59
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:49,130
What do you think mattered most: the mountains, the soldiers, or the diplomacy? Tell me in the comments.

60
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:55,980
Next time, we'll meet those soldiers up close: who the Gurkhas really are, and why armies feared them.

61
00:05:56,430 --> 00:05:58,750
This is Himalaya Unfolded.

